  "query_preview": "..A trough is deepening and moving into central\nCalifornia which will bring widespread precipitation to most of \nthe CWA tomorrow. This storm will quickly exit Tuesday night with\ndrier conditions for Wednesday. Another more long lasting storm is\nslated to arrive on Thursday with more widespread precipitation. A\nflattened ridge builds into the area for the weekend providing a\nsmall break in the action.\n\nThe approaching trough will bring scattered snow showers in\nsouthwestern Utah overnight tonight with snow level above 9000\nfeet. The baroclinic zone will bring a north to south band of heavier\nprecipitation, in addition to falling snow levels, into the area \nby sunrise on Tuesday morning with the most widespread activity\noccurring during the day on Tuesday. WPC has issued a marginal\nexcessive rainfall outlook for portions of southwestern Utah\nhighlighting the potential for rain rates up to 0.5\"/hour. Given\nthe record high soil moisture content across southwestern Utah\nareal flooding will be possible, although limited given the\nrapidly decreasing snow levels that will hold a lot of moisture in\nthe higher terrain in the form of snowpack.\n\nThe trough has trended colder over the past couple of runs. Prior\nensemble median temperatures brought in -8C 700mb temps, but now \nthose temperatures are coming in at -10C. This is increasing the\npotential for snow to get as low as valley floors after sunset on\nTuesday across northern Utah. The dynamics of this negatively\ntilted trough start to enhance as it moves into Utah. It reaches\npeak intensity as a vorticity lobe moves through and a \ndeformation zone and trowal potentially develop across northern \nUtah. This can be seen in many model soundings that show enhanced \nlift across the DGZ Tuesday evening with northwesterly winds \ndeveloping near the surface and a moist southwesterly flow \nremaining aloft. Even though surface temperatures will likely \nremain above freezing while it's precipitating across valley \nlocations there could be high enough precip rates to force snow \nlevels down to valley floors. This window will be short-lived, but\na quick inch or two of snow will be possible along with road \nslush for eastern benches and the Wasatch Back. \n\nMountain locations across the central and southern mountains will\nsee a shorter duration of snowfall which will limit snow totals to\n3-7 inches, with higher totals near 9 inches near Brian head. The\nnorthern mountains are favored given the first round of\nprecipitation associated with the frontal passage and the back end\ndeformation zone precipitation. 5-10 inches of snow is expected\nfor mountains above 8000 feet with locally higher totals of 12-20\ninches possible in the upper Cottonwoods.\n\nThis activity winds down overnight Tuesday as transient ridging\nredevelops across Utah and southwest Wyoming. The coldest air of\nthe season will filter in Tuesday night with most locations\noutside of lower Washington county below freezing. \n\nA broader and longer duration trough moves into the area by\nThursday with a trend towards colder 700 mb temperatures (-10C)\nthat could signal more valley snowfall possible in addition to\nmore widespread mountain snowfall with up to 12\"+ across the\nhigher terrain.",
